hi there it's cusco  and uh i haven't been able to put up  many videos  because very often the wi-fi isn't very  good  and so i can't upload them but  and i didn't sleep very well last night  must be because of the  high elevation here in cusco  and but i stepped in away from the noise  of the street to this courtyard  so that i could do a little video and  update you and  so we've had nine days in argentina  nine days  in brazil and now we're starting our  peruvian adventure  and of course one of the things is  argentina speaks spanish  brazil speaks portuguese and  here in peru of course they speak  spanish again  so i speak spanish a lot better than i  speak portuguese  and of course a big difficulty in both  spanish and portuguese  is the verbs  the verb endings  tenses  and knowing  which form of the verb it is in some  cases  and being able to produce it correctly  while speaking  so i thought i would share some of my  approaches to this so obviously i have a  lot more trouble in portuguese than i  have in spanish  um  the endings are different in portuguese  they also have  verbs that aren't used in spanish like  um i know ashar lambrades  and so  what ends up happening is that you're  speaking and you're never sure at least  i am not sure as i'm speaking portuguese  is this a spanish word or is this a  portuguese word so i have doubts  and this is the correct ending or not  the correct ending and i have these  doubts and doubts are very bad because  it then holds you back very often your  gut instinct  is correct if you just go with it rather  than  doubting whether it's the correct form  or the correct word  and even if you get it wrong  it doesn't really matter people  understand what you're saying  and also very often you are aware that  you doubt it or you weren't totally sure  or maybe you got it wrong  and that at least in my case heightens  my  awareness of that word or form so then  what happens is when i'm reading and of  course i do a lot of reading here i've  bought books on argentinian history  brazilian history peruvian history  so i'm reading and wherever i see a word  a verb form  or other phrases but typically mostly  verb forms that i'm not totally sure of  i underline them in the book  if i am on link which i  was doing a lot of for portuguese  then what i really recommend and i think  i've mentioned this before is context  reversal so i will look up a verb  which appeared in a certain form in a  sentence i may even know that verb so  i'm not really looking for the meaning  of that verb but i open up  the the link widget  and immediately go to context reversal  because what context reverso does  it highlights that'll give you like the  whole  all the conjugations of that verb  but it will highlight the one that you  chose the one that's in that text  so it kind of does two things first of  all it tells you what form that is  it highlights it in all of the  conjugation tables plus in order to get  to that you kind of have a quick review  of the other  um you know tenses and and persons and  so forth and so on and so doing these  things whether you're on link and you're  using context reversal or just reading  in a book and underlying verb forms  i find that this  gradually makes you  better  and and yet when you go to speak you  still have to just go with it not doubt  yourself  just say whatever you want to say and  hope it's right  but i think this sort of  poking at the verb either by underlying  it in a book or if you're on link and  you're accessing context reverso or even  if you're  not reading on link but you're on a  website somewhere you can access context  reversal and see  which is the appropriate which is the  actual form that you were looking at  gradually builds up your ability to  produce it correctly when you need it so  that's what i wanted to say  and i'm going to go and have some  breakfast now and try to upload this  hopefully the wi-fi at the hotel is good  enough  i should add that  really enjoying it here so far in peru  love the people very energetic they seem  uh friendly the brazilians especially in  rio were just amazing in their  enthusiastic friendliness  uh  in salvador  equally so although a bit of a negative  up there is the over aggressive people  selling their wares in the old town  uh but by and large it's been a  wonderful trip and um  yeah i'm looking forward to  uh wandering around cusco today and then  tomorrow we're going to machu picchu bye  for now  [Music]
 

